---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How we do this"
  description="How Gridwatch turns public electricity files into a daily website."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How we do this</h1>
    <p>
      The numbers on this site come from files published by the Electricity
      Authority, the public body in charge of New Zealand's electricity market.
      The Authority puts them on a site called Electricity Market Information,
      or EMI. Those files say who generated power, and what it sold for, in the
      wholesale market: the trading layer between power stations and the
      retailers who send you a bill.
    </p>
    <p>
      We download those files and tidy them, then build the pages from a day
      that has already finished.
    </p>
    <p>
      We keep three stages of the data, labelled bronze, silver, and gold.
      Bronze is the original download, kept exactly as it was published. Silver
      is those same records with the dates and names made consistent. Gold is
      the numbers we actually put on the site, such as daily generation and
      prices, built from that silver data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once a day we generate ordinary web pages from those gold files and
      publish them. There isn't a live connection to the market, so if a price
      spike is still happening, it won't be here yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      We stop at what already happened. Tomorrow's prices aren't in these files,
      and you shouldn't use the site to operate the high-voltage network.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }
</style>
